        <h3 class="titile">About the Company</h3>
        <p>
            <span class="redheader"><b>Hindustan Copper Limited (HCL)</b></span> is a Schedule &lsquo;A&rsquo; Miniratna, Category-I Central
            Public Sector Enterprise (CPSE). It works under the Ministry of Mines, Government of India.
        </p>
        <p>
            HCL was incorporated in 1967 under the Companies Act, 1956. It is the only company in India
            that mines copper ore. It holds all the operating mining leases for copper ore in the country.
        </p>

        <h3 class="titile">What HCL Does</h3>
        <p>
            HCL explores for copper and mines it. It then processes the ore to raise its copper content
            (this is called beneficiation). The result is copper concentrate, which HCL sells.
        </p>

        <h3 class="titile">Facilities</h3>
        <p>HCL has the following mines and plants:</p>
        <ul class="profile-facility-list">
            <li><strong>Copper mines:</strong> Malanjkhand (Madhya Pradesh), Khetri (Rajasthan) and Ghatsila (Jharkhand).</li>
            <li><strong>Primary smelter and refinery:</strong> Ghatsila (Jharkhand).</li>
            <li><strong>Secondary smelter and refinery:</strong> Jhagadia (Gujarat).</li>
            <li><strong>Continuous Cast Copper Wire Rod plant:</strong> Taloja (Maharashtra).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The Ghatsila and Jhagadia smelting and refining facilities have been suspended since 2019
            because of business considerations.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Taloja plant currently does only tolling work. This means it processes material for
            other companies (third parties) for a fee.
        </p>

        <h3 class="titile">Workforce and Registered Office</h3>
        <p>
            As of 1 April 2026, HCL had 1,154 regular employees. Of these, 552 are executives and
            602 are non-executives.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Registered and Corporate Office is at Tamra Bhavan, 1 Ashutosh Chowdhury Avenue,
            Kolkata, West Bengal, PIN 700019.
        </p>

        <h3 class="titile">Capital and Ownership</h3>
        <p>
            As of 31 March 2026, the authorised capital of the company was <i class="fa fa-inr" aria-hidden="true"></i>1,100 crore.
            The paid-up capital was <i class="fa fa-inr" aria-hidden="true"></i>483.51 crore.
        </p>
        <p>
            The Government of India holds 66.14% of the company&rsquo;s equity (shares).
        </p>

        <h3 class="titile">Financial Performance</h3>
        <p>
            In FY 2025&ndash;26, HCL earned a Profit Before Tax (PBT) of <i class="fa fa-inr" aria-hidden="true"></i>1,232.73 crore
            on net sales of <i class="fa fa-inr" aria-hidden="true"></i>3,054.40 crore.
        </p>
        <p>
            In the six months ended September 2025, HCL earned a PBT of <i class="fa fa-inr" aria-hidden="true"></i>427.99 crore
            on net sales of <i class="fa fa-inr" aria-hidden="true"></i>1,216.19 crore.
        </p>
        <p>
            HCL has paid dividends to its shareholders every year since FY 2020&ndash;21.
        </p>

